# Session sharing settings for this build (read by run.sh, so it covers run-openai.sh too).
#
# SYNC_CLAUDE_SESSIONS=1 -> the session store is SHARED with Claude Desktop, as a symlink:
#
#   <repo>/user-data/claude-code-sessions
#     -> ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude-code-sessions
#
# Each local_<uuid>.json is one session's metadata — sessionId, cliSessionId, cwd, title,
# titleSource, isArchived, model, effort, permissionMode, enabledMcpTools, lastActivityAt.
#
# This used to be a one-way `rsync --update` copy, which is why issue #3 existed: sessions
# created or renamed HERE had nowhere to go, so the two stores drifted apart in both
# directions. By the time it was changed there were 13 sessions only in the real install,
# 64 only in this build, and 15 that differed. Sharing removes the whole problem — there is
# one directory, so nothing to copy, nothing to merge, and nothing to go stale.
#
# Safe to share because these are plain per-session JSON files: no lock, no database, and
# both sides already use the identical <user>/<org> path so the link needs no translation.
# Local Storage canNOT be shared the same way — see SYNC_CLAUDE_GROUPING below for why.
#
# TWO CONSEQUENCES, both deliberate:
#
#  1. This build now WRITES into the real install's data. Everything else in user-data/ is
#     still isolated; this one directory is not. Verified in both directions: a file created
#     under user-data/claude-code-sessions/ appears at the real install path and vice versa.
#
#  2. Deletion is GLOBAL. `isArchived` is 0 across every session file on both sides, so the
#     UI removes a session by deleting its file — and it is now one file, not two. Removing
#     a session in either app removes it from both.
#
# Before the symlink can be created, anything unique to this build has to be merged into the
# shared store, or it would be abandoned. run.sh refuses to link while that is true and tells
# you to run `node scripts/merge-sessions.mjs` (use --dry-run first; it backs both trees up
# to user-data/claude-code-sessions.premerge and prints a per-file decision table).
#
# NOT shared, because it does not need to be: the agent's config and memory are ALREADY
# common. Both apps resolve CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR to $HOME/.claude, so this build reads the
# same ~/.claude/projects/*/memory/*.md, MEMORY.md, CLAUDE.md, settings and session
# transcripts. Verified: a session created in this build wrote its transcript to
# ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<cliSessionId>.jsonl, and its child processes carry
# HOME=$HOME with no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR override.
#
# TWO stores are shared, both through the same share_store() helper in run.sh:
#
#   claude-code-sessions        one local_<uuid>.json per session
#   Claude Extensions           installed DXT / MCP extensions, plus their settings. Nothing
#                               blocked them here (isDesktopExtensionEnabled: true, signature
#                               not required) — the directory was simply never copied, so the
#                               real install's extensions were invisible to this build.
#   local-agent-mode-sessions   the cowork / agent-mode store: the same per-session json PLUS
#                               a local_<uuid>/ workspace directory each, the skills-plugin
#                               payload, and server-refreshed cowork-*-cache.json files
#
# The second was checked before being shared: 373 files on both sides with identical file
# lists, and exactly 2 files differing — the skills and rpm manifest.json, and only in their
# `lastUpdated` refresh timestamp. So there was nothing to merge. Its .lock files are per-task
# files inside session workspaces, not a database lock, so sharing is safe the same way.
#
# Set to 0 to launch without touching either store. An existing symlink is left
# in place — remove it by hand if you want the profiles separate again, and note that doing
# so leaves this build with NO sessions until you copy some back.
SYNC_CLAUDE_SESSIONS=1

# SYNC_CLAUDE_GROUPING -> merge the claude.ai sidebar GROUPING state, both ways.
#
# Runs scripts/sync-grouping.mjs at launch and again after the app quits. It touches exactly
# three keys in the claude.ai origin's Local Storage:
#
#   dframe-store -> state.customGroupsByScope   THE authority the sidebar reads. Per account
#                                         scope: the groups (cg-<uuid> + name), the assignments
#                                         map saying which session is in which group, and the
#                                         per-group session order.
#   dframe-store -> state.groupByByMode   Without this the groups exist but the sidebar does not
#                                         group by them.
#   LSS-persisted.dframe-group-scopes     A LEGACY MIRROR of the same model. Written to stay
#                                         consistent, never read into the merge.
#   LSS-persisted.starred-session-groups  starred groups
#
# Nothing else in dframe-store is touched. sidebarWidth (389 there, 280 here) and collapsedGroups
# are per-window preferences and stay that way.
#
# WHICH KEY IS AUTHORITATIVE — CORRECTING WHAT THIS FILE SAID TWICE BEFORE.
#
# First it said grouping was a server-side store (GET /api/claude_code/organizations/{org}/
# user_settings -> customGroups / customGroupAssignments / customGroupOrder) that only mirrored
# into Local Storage, so nothing local could be synced. Wrong: those server fields are empty for
# this account while the sidebar is fully grouped, and the two apps disagreed with each other,
# which a shared server store cannot produce.
#
# Then it said LSS-persisted.dframe-group-scopes was that local source of truth. Also wrong, and
# caught by the app itself: writing the merge into that key while the app was closed verified
# fine, then the app launched and put its own value straight back — same key, fresh timestamp,
# new tabId, merge gone. It is a legacy mirror the app regenerates from its store. At the same
# moment the two disagreed INSIDE one profile: the mirror held 7 groups, the store held 8.
#
# The authority is dframe-store.state.customGroupsByScope. Merging that survives a relaunch —
# verified: this build went from 61 to 71 assignments and kept them across a boot, gaining 4
# sessions in App Analysis, 4 in Prod Routines and the 2 that made "email quality" appear at all.
#
# WHY IT IS A MERGE AND NOT A COPY. The whole-directory copy this replaced was wrong in two ways.
# It replaced all ~371 of the destination's claude.ai keys — every composer draft, sidebar
# preference and the account bootstrap cache — to fix 3 of them, which is why it had to default
# to off. And being one-way it discarded whatever the destination had done itself: measured on the
# authoritative key, the real install had 8 groups / 69 assignments and this build 8 / 61, with 10
# assignments only in the real install AND 2 only in the build. Either direction of copy loses
# real work. The union is 8 / 71, and the two assignment sets were disjoint, so the merge
# arbitrates nothing away. Genuine conflicts would go to the newer LSS- mirror timestamp — a weak
# clock, since the app rewrites the mirror at launch, but it only ever decides a true tie.
#
# DELETIONS DO NOT PROPAGATE, by design. "Absent on one side" is indistinguishable from "not yet
# added there", and there is no per-entry deletion clock. Remove a group in one app and the next
# merge restores it from the other. Remove it in both, or accept that it comes back.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL NOT A SYMLINK, unlike the session store above. LevelDB allows exactly one
# process at a time. Both databases hold an exclusive whole-file fcntl(F_WRLCK) on their LOCK
# file for as long as their app runs — verified directly with F_GETLK:
#
#   .../Claude/Local Storage/leveldb/LOCK      F_WRLCK  (Claude Desktop)
#   .../user-data/Local Storage/leveldb/LOCK   F_WRLCK  (this build)
#
# Point both apps at one directory and the second to start cannot open its Local Storage at all.
# A native LevelDB binding does not help: it takes the same lock on the same inode. So a profile
# can only be WRITTEN while its own app is closed. Reads dodge this entirely by working from a
# directory snapshot, which touches no lock — that is why the merge can always be computed, and
# why only the writing half is conditional. Each profile is written if its app is closed and
# skipped with a note if not, so:
#
#   * at launch, this build is always written (it is by definition not running yet)
#   * after quit, this build is written again, and Claude Desktop too if you have it closed
#   * with Claude Desktop open the whole time, its share of the merge waits for the next run
#
# Requires node and the classic-level package (a devDependency: npm install). With either
# missing the step is skipped and the launch continues.
#
# Set to 0 to leave both profiles' grouping alone.
#
# WARNING, learned the hard way: replacing or deleting Local Storage DOES break login. An
# earlier version of this comment claimed it did not, on the reasoning that authentication lives
# in Cookies. Deleting Local Storage produced 401s and a failed bootstrap until the directory was
# restored, so the desktop keeps auth state there too. This is why the merge writes individual
# keys and never replaces the directory — and why it still backs the whole directory up to
# "<profile>/Local Storage.grouping-bak" before writing, and verifies by reading back.
#
# Also: never restore a LevelDB backup taken from a profile that was not closed cleanly. A backup
# copied after the app was killed with pkill left the app hanging in window setup — renderers
# reporting "Terminating current process after 15 seconds with no connection" — because the copy
# captured a torn write. The recovery is to copy a known-good Local Storage in, not to delete it.
SYNC_CLAUDE_GROUPING=1
